@flexiarg math-formalization-CA/laurent-clearing-to-algebraic-polynomial
@title Clear Laurent Negative Powers to Reach an Algebraic Polynomial
@sigils [🌀/映]
@keywords Laurent, unit circle, trigonometric polynomial, Polynomial, roots, argument principle, winding
@audience lean-provers, complex-analysis
@tone heuristic
@difficulty hard
@subjects complex-analysis
@instantiates math-informal/reduce-to-known-result
@examples a95J03

! conclusion:
  When the object is a finite Laurent sum over Icc (-N) N evaluated on the
  unit circle, multiply through by w ^ N to clear the negative powers and
  work with an ordinary Polynomial of degree at most 2N, where the whole
  root-multiplicity API (roots, rootMultiplicity, card_roots) applies.

  + IF: The goal speaks about a trigonometric/Laurent expression
    sum over Icc (-N) N of a n * w ^ n with w on Metric.sphere 0 1, and
    Mathlib has no direct integer-valued winding/index API for it.

  + HOWEVER: Polynomial lemmas (degree bounds, root counts, divisor sums)
    do not apply to Laurent monomials with negative exponents.

  + THEN: Define the cleared polynomial sum over Finset.range (2 * N + 1)
    with coefficients a (k - N); prove eval w = w ^ N * Laurent-sum via
    Finset.sum_bij (k ↦ k - N) between range (2N+1) and Icc (-N) N,
    using zpow_natCast / zpow_add₀ to relate natural and integer powers at
    w ≠ 0. Nonvanishing of the Laurent sum - z on the circle transfers
    exactly to nonvanishing of the cleared polynomial on the sphere, since
    w ^ N never vanishes there.
    + LEAN:
      api[Finset.sum_bij] api[zpow_add₀] api[zpow_natCast]
      api[Polynomial.card_roots'] api[Polynomial.natDegree_sum_le_of_forall_le]

  + BECAUSE: The factor w ^ N is a unit on the unit circle, so zeros,
    logarithmic derivatives, and winding contributions shift only by the
    explicit constant N, which the caller can subtract by hand.
